@lusoris thank you, that's a really thorough validation. The padded writable layer is a nice way to force the AutoRemove window, and seeing the exited reaper never waited on in 14 of 14 forced runs is exactly the behaviour #3868 is meant to pin.

On the 20 s limit: that's the existing backoff for reaper creation (`MaxElapsedTime` in `reaperSpawner`), which already bounded the name-conflict retries before this PR. The fix reuses it rather than adding a second timer, so a removal slower than 20 s would have failed on v0.44.0 too, just with a different error. If someone does hit that in practice it would be easy to make the bound configurable, but as you say real removals are two orders of magnitude below it.

I'll link your results from the PR so the maintainers have them when they get to it.

@ould-a Thanks for looking into this! 🙂 
I didn't write/test this module (only reviewed) so I'm catching up with what you find.

Quick replies:
1. Technically, `chunk_size` and `block_size_fit` are equal and represent the same thing, as defined [here](https://github.com/GlacioHack/xdem/blob/3e2294775d1dfb2c7a041ec312f0e8a78f25403f/xdem/coreg/blockwise.py#L107). If I understand your point correctly, the **chunk boundaries** (tuple of xmin/ymin/xmax/ymax) (that can differ near the edges of the DEM, if the DEM shape is not an exact multiple of `chunk_size`) will not necessarily align with the way the centers are currently defined? Is that the issue?

2. Repeating `chunk_size`: The reason behind this is that `BlockwiseCoreg` does not necessarily have to run in Multiprocessing (at least that the design we aim for, like for all the other features).  It could simply run in a for loop without parallelization (no `mp_config` passed). Or, it could run each block out-of-memory respecting the array chunks (`chunk_size` smaller than `fit_block_size`), which will be available once we link the GeoUtils functionalities. So `chunk_size != block_size` (or one can be undefined) in the general case. It could definitely default to the same when `chunk_size` exists, though.

@belletva That reminds me that a lot of things we planned to modify in `BlockwiseCoreg` are on hold since Alice stopped? We need to discuss this tonight. The vision was clear for me there but it was a bit difficult to iterate

@mdelapenya after some more digging I have some code that can reproduce (I tried with the same image in the tests but that's not the issue). The code to reproduce:
```golang
package experiment

import (
	"context"
	"testing"

	"github.com/docker/docker/api/types/container"
	"github.com/stretchr/testify/require"
	"github.com/testcontainers/testcontainers-go"
	"github.com/testcontainers/testcontainers-go/modules/redpanda"
)

func TestRedpanda(t *testing.T) {
	t.Parallel()

	ctx := context.Background()

	redpandaContainer, err := redpanda.Run(ctx,
		"docker.redpanda.com/redpandadata/redpanda:latest",
		testcontainers.WithConfigModifier(func(config *container.Config) {
			// Setting this config modifier overrides the existing one which applies the user
			// In my own code, I was just trying to add some labels here
		}),
	)
	require.NoError(t, err)

	defer func() {
		require.NoError(t, testcontainers.TerminateContainer(redpandaContainer))
	}()
}
```

The cause seems to be that I was using `testcontainers.WithConfigModifier`, which I was doing just to add some labels. This seems to replace the original configuration from the screenshot above in the main code.

Would you say this is expected behaviour? My intention wasn't to replace the entire config modifier originally, just add to it...
